<h1>Nípa Ọ̀NÀ</h1>
<p class="lede-block">Ọ̀NÀ ("the path") is an archive of Yoruba words, proverbs, Ifá/Odù, and culture — built from years of personal fieldwork, published sources, and community research, with every entry's origin and confidence level shown honestly rather than hidden.</p>

<h2>Bí a ṣe ń ṣàmì àwọn àkọsílẹ̀ — How entries are marked</h2>
<ul class="about-list">
  <li><span class="badge verified">Sourced / Fieldwork verified</span> — collected via direct fieldwork with informant consent, or cited to a published academic source.</li>
  <li><span class="badge partial">Fieldwork (partial)</span> — collected in the field but not yet fully cross-checked against a second source.</li>
  <li><span class="badge unverified">Community-sourced — unverified</span> — added through web research to fill a content gap (e.g. Ifá/Odù coverage), cross-checked where possible but not yet confirmed by a language authority.</li>
  <li><span class="badge unverified">AI research — unverified</span> — an AI-assisted research brief from a single source document, kept separate from fieldwork- and publication-sourced content.</li>
  <li><span class="badge disputed">Disputed sources</span> — sources actively disagree; shown honestly rather than resolved artificially.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Kí ni Ọ̀NÀ kì í ṣe — What this isn't</h2>
<p>This is a living, growing archive, not a finished or authoritative reference. Some entries are thin; some are unverified; help is welcome — via the Telegram bot or the contacts below.</p>

